From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:00:23 -0400
Subject: add the Space switcher to the sidebar

A row of pills, one per Space, with the active one filled in that Space's
colour, and a button that makes a new Space and switches to it. Clicking a pill
changes the active Space, which changes which tabs the sidebar shows.

Two things the layout taught us, both now in comments rather than only in the
code: the sidebar lays its children out vertically and stretches anything that
does not declare a preferred size, so the row says it is exactly one pill tall;
and the very top of the sidebar belongs to the window's caption buttons on
macOS, so the switcher sits under the sidebar's own buttons rather than above
them, where the first attempt put it underneath the traffic lights.

The row rebuilds itself on any model change. It is at most a handful of
buttons, and reconciling would be more code and more ways to be wrong than
rebuilding when Spaces change.

A new Space is made unnamed, taking the next colour from a small palette so two
made in a row do not look alike, and becomes active immediately -- which is the
point of making one, since the next tab opened lands in it.

Why: without this there is no way to reach a second Space, and Spaces are the
feature people name when they say why they stay in Arc.
Removable when: never, short of dropping Spaces.
